            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;



Overall, the air leakage from your home is quite high, and there are some key locations where this exists.



- The crawlspace below is heated space with some ductwork feeding heat down there. The crawlspace is definitely not an air tight location of the home, as we could feel air coming up through the floor heat ducts on the main floor. Please see my section about insulation the crawlspace as I talk about the pros &amp; cons of heating that crawlspace.



- There appears to be 2 combustion air pipes being fed into the furnace room. One is the tall pipe that extends up to the ceiling behind the furnace. I believe the other one is the floor vent in front of the furnace (likely the original one) and it appears that one is still drawing air in from outside. You should have your heating contractor determine how much combustion air is truly required for that furnace and eliminate any excess pipes. Ideally, your heating contractor would install an electronic damper on the remaining combustion air pipe so that it only opens whenever the furnace comes on.



- Many of the older windows do not seal well &amp; are sources of air leakage &amp; heat loss.



- The rear door requires an update to the weather-stripping.



- The attic hatch requires weather-stripping or could be sealed with a removable caulking (eg: Draft Attack).



- The bathroom exhaust fans appear to allow heat loss even when the fans are not operating. You may want to consider upgrading this fan to a new Energy Star model that have good butterfly dampers, uses less electricity &amp; operates alot quieter.



- Some other smaller air leakage in your house is from around &amp; through many of the electrical outlets &amp; wall switches located on exterior walls. For these electrical outlets, you can install the specially designed foam gaskets behind the cover plates.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

The software predicts approximately a 13% reduction is easily achieved in your house, where you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 508&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11.7GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. Personally, I think you can achieve a 20% - 30% reduction if that crawlspace leakage can be effectively resolved ......... or perhaps eliminate the heating of that crawlspace. Of course, this requires protection of the water pipes &amp; heat ducts in the crawlspace &amp; the floor above the crawlspace needs to be fully air sealed !!



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appears to have 2 layers with a 2x4 (R12) inner wall plus an exterior section that I assumed to have another R8 of insulation. For your longer term planning, I have modeled the expected energy savings if you were to add 2.5" (R12.5) of rigid foam insulation to the exterior of these walls in conjunction with a future exterior siding upgrade.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls of the house with an additional R12.5 insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 305&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house only has the original R12 fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled increasing this to a minimum R50 with either additional batt of blown fibreglass insulation .......... while protecting the ventilation of the attic at the eaves by including baffles between the rafters to ensure the new insulation does not block the air flow.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing the attic insulation from R12 up to a minimum R50, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,242&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;28.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

The crawlspace foundation of your house is currently heated space and appears to be one of the most significant locations of heat loss from your home. Most crawlspaces in Norman Wells are unheated space, where there are no exposed water pipes that could freeze, the floor above is well insulated and air sealed and any heating system ductwork running through the crawlspace has all of the joints fully sealed and the ductwork is substantially insulated.



When a crawlspace is heated, the walls and floor of the crawlspace need to be well insulated and air tight. As the floor of your crawlspace is a dirt floor (and will still be significantly impacted by the ground temperatures outside) this floor will still represent a significant source of heat loss to your home. At a minimum, the floor needs to be covered by a full vapour barrier (sheet of heavy gauge plastic, with joints sealed) to prevent ground moisture from affecting this crawlspace with excess moisture and mold. During the late spring, summer &amp; early fall (the non heating season), this crawlspace should be well ventilated by installing wall vents and/or opening the crawlspace door.



Ideally, your longer term solution towards energy savings is to eliminate this crawlspace as "heated space" - as outlined in my first paragraph above. For the shorter term, you may just want to focus on reducing the air leakage from this crawlspace (through holes in the walls - around pipes, etc.) and increase the amount of wall insulation by adding another 4" (R20) of rigid foam insulation to either the interior ofd the walls or the exterior (in conjunction with adding more insulation to the exterior of the main floor walls above).



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing 100% of the exterior crawlspace walls of the house (including the header space between the crawlspace and the main floor above) with an addition R20 insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 473&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the total exterior wall area of the crawlspace walls and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</foundation>
                <windows>

Most of the windows in your home are older and many of them have broken hardware and do not close &amp; seal properly. For your longer term energy efficiency planning, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them to ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing the windows with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 152&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. As discussed, the payback on windows (from strictly an energy savings perspective) is quite long, however most homeowners consider upgrading for other reasons - current windows have broken hardware or frames, the seals between the glass have deteriorated, wanting to switch from sliders to casement, etc. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 182 litre electric hot water tank, and the cost of electricity in Norman Wells is very expensive. In fact, electricity in Norman Wells is generated primarily using diesel and the efficiency of the diesel generators is often less than if you used the oil directly to create the hot water. I did see in this house that it used to have a gas hot water heater (that was vented up a chimney), however it was replaced with an electric hot water tank in conjunction with the conversion away from natural gas in Norman Wells a few years ago.



The Arctic Energy Alliance is starting to see a variety of higher efficiency oil hot water heaters being installed in all regions of the Northwest Territories (most are direct vent through a side wall, rather than up a chimney). Although we do not recommend or endorse any manufacturers or products, I will mention a unit that we are seeing more NWT contractors starting to install - the Toyotomi OM-122HW (and I saw some in Norman Wells). We are advised by Natural Resources Canada that this unit is not currently Canadian ENERGY STARÂ® rated, however they indicate it has an Energy Factor of 0.682.



The AEA adjudicates the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP) and the current guidelines indicate that an instantaneous on-demand oil hot water heater must have a minimum 0.68 EF and be Canadian ENERGY STARÂ® rated. As such, the Toyotomi OM-122HW is not eligible for the $1,250 EEIP rebate ........... however we are currently reviewing this with the GNWT - so please call me or check our website for an update. 





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your hot electric hot water tank with an instantaneous oil-fired water heater with a 0.682 EF, there would be a small increase in overall energy use, however the cost difference between oil &amp; electricity indicates that you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save up to $ 987&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. As mentioned above, this upgrade does not currently qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil furnace is an older concentric flue (direct vent) model that is rated at 80% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE). It also has a 4" fresh air supply pipe connected at the top to bring in fresh air to the house (whenever an exhaust fan is operating), and the furnace fan then evenly distributes the fresh air throughout the house.



As you have an older single wall oil tank, we talked about options and costs you may want to consider. Some of these include the cost to upgrade your oil tank to a double wall "containment" tank, the cost &amp; efficiency of a new oil furnace &amp; perhaps the insurance surcharge you pay when an oil tank is on your property. As new oil furnaces are generally between 85% - 87% AFUE, we also talked about the option of looking at a new 95% AFUE propane furnace, with a high efficiency ECM motor.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current oil furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 546&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;14.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



As discussed while I was at your house, the Arctic Energy Alliance always recommends homeowners complete all energy conservation (eg: LED Bulbs, timer switches, etc.) &amp; energy efficiency (eg: heating &amp; hot water systems) measures before investing in renewable energy generation systems.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the back South facing roof of your house, flush mounted on the roof, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save about $ 866&lt;/b&gt; per year in electricity costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 5,000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $5,000 per year in a thermal electricity community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets and one of them is an older 13 litre model, and you should consider replacing it with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



This house has multiple components comprising the ventilation design:



1. A kitchen range hood fan that vents to the outside.



2. A bathroom exhaust fan, operated by a regular switch. An easy upgrade is to install a timer switch (5 -10-15-20 minutes) so the fan will be operated long enough to exhaust odours &amp; humidity, however cannot be accidentally left on all day, wasting large amounts of heated air from your house.



3. The clothes dryer is vented to the outside. Please note the dryer vent has fallen down in the crawlspace to there is a big dip in the pipe &amp; this will promote a buildup of water &amp; lint in the pipe, causing restricted air flow (so clothes do not dry properly) and can potentially create a fire hazard.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (kitchen range hood, bathroom fan, and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.1 Pascal. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment would not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home. The furnace does currently vent up a chimney and is not currently at risk of back-drafting combustion fumes into the house.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be a functioning smoke detector, however the carbon monoxide (CO) detector had the battery removed. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date (normally every 10 years). Your current detectors appeared to be older &amp; in need of replacement.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Norman Wells&lt;/b&gt;



The following Nov. 1, 2017 values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Norman Wells:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh up to the TPSP threshold, $0.54 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.52 per litre 

Wood - $472.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.14 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



